[{:db/id 17592186198369 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b5a18f4e-2d75-5904-b5e5-ab7ba2838493"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3e5df847-265a-5b9c-9720-3136b8ccb366" "question-fa0b27c3-d4dd-5c0c-8989-66af8729df06"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198377 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ff752515-0aa8-5668-9f6f-2eed5f67dc87" "question-22e295c4-59c2-57da-8c38-5051b16dc6ee"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198375 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b54a0f96-7cef-5900-9667-b793d1ee0611" "question-9b6d5f3b-6c7c-587f-b086-0bc40be20551"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198017 :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we have\n\n$$\nx = u \\cos v, \\qquad y = u \\sin v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $\\Delta$ of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  \\sqrt 2 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{12}  \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=\\sqrt 2$ and $u=3,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ and $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c8/c83bd9d913d4959ad275ab4420b647cd030fff45438f59d026e3658bd146a1ee.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186278346 :question/problem "![](images/04/0412da40e0e04e8da5ec92db1ad665251209a718333ec008ceeebbe308c71855.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186389098 :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e0e9b7d2cbff011a5ff209f335fb3d5e86af02a58c4ea5c8a8d46bd384e6a0c.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the right side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b5a18f4e-2d75-5904-b5e5-ab7ba2838493" :question/id #uuid "b5a18f4e-2d75-5904-b5e5-ab7ba2838493" :question/math-academy-id "q-160968" :question/problem "![](images/93/93132c9352ed5068001c824741a1c0a7cb8dca82564ad91db706bc1ecfcedbcc.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 6,\\,\\,\\frac{14\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the right side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the right side.\n\nFor the right side, we have $u=6$ and $\\frac{14\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=6$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{14\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $6$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the fourth quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{14\\pi }{9}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/b6/b6052c625de5a1d392975afae8c1053b2b430859ba6fbc3bc8c2c3de01d40318.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8362e8f8-2931-5481-95c5-afb548403e1c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8362e8f8-2931-5481-95c5-afb548403e1c" :answer-field/id #uuid "8362e8f8-2931-5481-95c5-afb548403e1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-82b1b19a-a349-584f-a6da-2df60e51ccbd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a10f5005-7b34-598d-9b41-d0cd54665e85" "answer-b1629f56-2fe8-5dd2-9891-1c223dd671bf" "answer-5f3549c5-ab21-5d1a-90d8-9ee9169a75ea" "answer-82b1b19a-a349-584f-a6da-2df60e51ccbd" "answer-8e06a460-44eb-5e1a-a4e7-d7c2cfd8e89e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a10f5005-7b34-598d-9b41-d0cd54665e85" :answer/id #uuid "a10f5005-7b34-598d-9b41-d0cd54665e85" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/51/51344600fafe3b69a5a055bae6b5691ae72f7af923eef64062f4502a0c6dbc2d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1629f56-2fe8-5dd2-9891-1c223dd671bf" :answer/id #uuid "b1629f56-2fe8-5dd2-9891-1c223dd671bf" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/88/882d8af547164ee97fde649bd178a6c57d78176e2f40b0878ee8dcb0cf61aa68.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f3549c5-ab21-5d1a-90d8-9ee9169a75ea" :answer/id #uuid "5f3549c5-ab21-5d1a-90d8-9ee9169a75ea" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/ac/ac62c64d3a078be5432e7cdca17212226d7f11924878678aeef7b1fc18ebed07.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82b1b19a-a349-584f-a6da-2df60e51ccbd" :answer/id #uuid "82b1b19a-a349-584f-a6da-2df60e51ccbd" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b6/b6052c625de5a1d392975afae8c1053b2b430859ba6fbc3bc8c2c3de01d40318.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e06a460-44eb-5e1a-a4e7-d7c2cfd8e89e" :answer/id #uuid "8e06a460-44eb-5e1a-a4e7-d7c2cfd8e89e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186278347 :question/problem "![](images/29/2932639855f3031da6adcca7eaee38b992a145809f119902e93958fee47ff2a6.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186278350 :question/problem "![](images/21/21952fe273fd1a1ecb03727f4aa254d4e0ecdab2feaeb1d8c652bc9923404846.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{13\\pi }{12}\\le v\\le \\frac{13\\pi }{9}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-3e5df847-265a-5b9c-9720-3136b8ccb366" :question/id #uuid "3e5df847-265a-5b9c-9720-3136b8ccb366" :question/math-academy-id "q-161024" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a44852c606e67842a58ad1a696253e6e211816f8243beec8b3eea97ef42055a2.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{9}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{9}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{9}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{9}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{9})),\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{9}$ with the positive $x$-axis and whose endpoints lie on circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ef04b3fa4765bbad1bd7345fe5217feac73c21251b99426c17ed22ec3bbd1.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cb72ef15-e2d9-5b72-84ec-e98850448e17"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cb72ef15-e2d9-5b72-84ec-e98850448e17" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb72ef15-e2d9-5b72-84ec-e98850448e17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0fa43f41-195e-5521-a4ae-5695230229e7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-33fa1164-e443-5847-962d-f5038cdc0390" "answer-4ba7decd-30d3-50fb-b574-0b6278fb2388" "answer-493f0a23-3da5-598b-a8a0-9acfa743f9c8" "answer-0fa43f41-195e-5521-a4ae-5695230229e7" "answer-332853ed-2acb-5257-876f-53cb0dc7f6d9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33fa1164-e443-5847-962d-f5038cdc0390" :answer/id #uuid "33fa1164-e443-5847-962d-f5038cdc0390" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1a/1a52a5fba437ff08657af756644f5d4569d35bd7598b92721423103d8c308ca2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ba7decd-30d3-50fb-b574-0b6278fb2388" :answer/id #uuid "4ba7decd-30d3-50fb-b574-0b6278fb2388" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/34/34be2d9120d93014b0d176aca946ebaf310ccdd574285c487c26a8fe00778412.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-493f0a23-3da5-598b-a8a0-9acfa743f9c8" :answer/id #uuid "493f0a23-3da5-598b-a8a0-9acfa743f9c8" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/74/74d029bf33b8c0e978006a0fb066843abf4c2dd0fc4c8ea7d125ae53b1626570.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fa43f41-195e-5521-a4ae-5695230229e7" :answer/id #uuid "0fa43f41-195e-5521-a4ae-5695230229e7" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/8c/8c5ef04b3fa4765bbad1bd7345fe5217feac73c21251b99426c17ed22ec3bbd1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-332853ed-2acb-5257-876f-53cb0dc7f6d9" :answer/id #uuid "332853ed-2acb-5257-876f-53cb0dc7f6d9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186278352 :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa62dda1abb7bc6bca1b2e6cdf356b52946820912ec6318e5901273a546ee96a.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-fa0b27c3-d4dd-5c0c-8989-66af8729df06" :question/id #uuid "fa0b27c3-d4dd-5c0c-8989-66af8729df06" :question/math-academy-id "q-161029" :question/problem "![](images/12/1252844f7a9e2fb2ca0ff1d2e2e1546a460e7d0846b4ae2996b6ce984ee305a1.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nI. The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nII. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nIII. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $1$ centered at $O.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ and $\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/49/49aa7d6ccd4b14b48ede7486f41ab11ac22b593c06d75795fe7819889b2b2f23.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\n- When $u=\\frac{1}{4},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f7528fa6-219c-52b3-82fe-4826326f572d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f7528fa6-219c-52b3-82fe-4826326f572d" :answer-field/id #uuid "f7528fa6-219c-52b3-82fe-4826326f572d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-45672c57-a19d-5a8b-ac6b-6ea21293b5d7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d1969766-6b47-59dc-a429-472653bbb6d3" "answer-c03a15a1-3e13-53ce-9c92-762f8bc2417d" "answer-45672c57-a19d-5a8b-ac6b-6ea21293b5d7" "answer-cc45f2dd-0a53-53f5-b140-3b2a83fa50cc" "answer-97210339-8c0b-5905-a2df-f3b57f435e8f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1969766-6b47-59dc-a429-472653bbb6d3" :answer/id #uuid "d1969766-6b47-59dc-a429-472653bbb6d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c03a15a1-3e13-53ce-9c92-762f8bc2417d" :answer/id #uuid "c03a15a1-3e13-53ce-9c92-762f8bc2417d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45672c57-a19d-5a8b-ac6b-6ea21293b5d7" :answer/id #uuid "45672c57-a19d-5a8b-ac6b-6ea21293b5d7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc45f2dd-0a53-53f5-b140-3b2a83fa50cc" :answer/id #uuid "cc45f2dd-0a53-53f5-b140-3b2a83fa50cc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97210339-8c0b-5905-a2df-f3b57f435e8f" :answer/id #uuid "97210339-8c0b-5905-a2df-f3b57f435e8f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198580 :question/problem "![](images/b8/b8874b14fa80881e69cb1daee180d2e342a27c5902e66fc75d43622278176b09.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nI. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nII. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nIII. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186278355 :question/problem "![](images/16/16d74107dbbf12749766cd9cea7e38d16e28a59efb997bd224aed73c7830324e.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{10\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nI. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{10\\pi }{9}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nII. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nIII. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186389077 :question/problem "![](images/95/951c3d3bca4116deebccbbabb5d7405bb07669cacb83c1e450204bb40c247d5c.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ff752515-0aa8-5668-9f6f-2eed5f67dc87" :question/id #uuid "ff752515-0aa8-5668-9f6f-2eed5f67dc87" :question/math-academy-id "q-160924" :question/problem "![](images/19/19f21fa7abb989e8c2bf35552675f498969e30e1f42012fd615c00686d20cb01.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{6\\pi }{7}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{7}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{7})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{6\\pi }{7}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/50/50462754e89259447fe464f3a9c8ca7f165260d4b7adfc25585912291091838b.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a914c4df-a2e3-591c-b430-880a8ee5c996"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a914c4df-a2e3-591c-b430-880a8ee5c996" :answer-field/id #uuid "a914c4df-a2e3-591c-b430-880a8ee5c996" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-caa435c1-c17e-509a-bc0c-1d20be69dbc8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8f0aeef6-20b2-5bbf-b82d-cf19738fc0b5" "answer-caa435c1-c17e-509a-bc0c-1d20be69dbc8" "answer-b0d258ef-d6de-5ab7-9099-98537e9ff4a4" "answer-1f7366f0-ea26-5a4d-ad99-5c479c1363cd" "answer-8f6dca9e-c430-5e66-b053-f24ceb1406ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f0aeef6-20b2-5bbf-b82d-cf19738fc0b5" :answer/id #uuid "8f0aeef6-20b2-5bbf-b82d-cf19738fc0b5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/4c/4cbda09d23f88ee9b7c87632180f10fbe0c678b3c8ac6b8b183bbc82fd43c774.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-caa435c1-c17e-509a-bc0c-1d20be69dbc8" :answer/id #uuid "caa435c1-c17e-509a-bc0c-1d20be69dbc8" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/50/50462754e89259447fe464f3a9c8ca7f165260d4b7adfc25585912291091838b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0d258ef-d6de-5ab7-9099-98537e9ff4a4" :answer/id #uuid "b0d258ef-d6de-5ab7-9099-98537e9ff4a4" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/90/909d48b33e74f7bdc0bd18d639325a88f7313126cadee7f367183541897e231e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f7366f0-ea26-5a4d-ad99-5c479c1363cd" :answer/id #uuid "1f7366f0-ea26-5a4d-ad99-5c479c1363cd" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/c8/c8890d6bf89507aff0334f26d0b906f260b3d5e00db974cdef59faf17d5068b1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f6dca9e-c430-5e66-b053-f24ceb1406ab" :answer/id #uuid "8f6dca9e-c430-5e66-b053-f24ceb1406ab" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/db/db98f6bc045f3f3a6bd7d22e061861b2d00182af6968b9562b59f22248d94f6b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-22e295c4-59c2-57da-8c38-5051b16dc6ee" :question/id #uuid "22e295c4-59c2-57da-8c38-5051b16dc6ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-160923" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f370177dc8119044b74fd4923c3d4318d5b9e1b4de89b63967284ee1cd7794da.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/03dae0fbacc0339613580414c2217652b9ff6303788c7ca265deb3b1f755f4cd.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d62a4acb-ad20-5c4f-b6b4-f3aea150ce3f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d62a4acb-ad20-5c4f-b6b4-f3aea150ce3f" :answer-field/id #uuid "d62a4acb-ad20-5c4f-b6b4-f3aea150ce3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-160c880d-89ca-54d9-91a4-23ab7095bd84" :answer-field/choices ["answer-94357291-cfe9-5d56-86c6-275da88ed2a1" "answer-2d848126-3113-59a0-965e-db5f69875346" "answer-160c880d-89ca-54d9-91a4-23ab7095bd84" "answer-7ff779d8-2902-5dbe-9b08-74d03fa4ab9b" "answer-28df0d1a-86ac-5691-b099-21f856eccfac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94357291-cfe9-5d56-86c6-275da88ed2a1" :answer/id #uuid "94357291-cfe9-5d56-86c6-275da88ed2a1" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/56/5663dfd0afc5b44954f73064b69aedcd33b983755fc7a425f4b963b3bad665eb.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d848126-3113-59a0-965e-db5f69875346" :answer/id #uuid "2d848126-3113-59a0-965e-db5f69875346" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/62/62156bad25518bbc8e9c324a2906ba804e6dc38edd37a752bd3092f2313ebea0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-160c880d-89ca-54d9-91a4-23ab7095bd84" :answer/id #uuid "160c880d-89ca-54d9-91a4-23ab7095bd84" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/97/97ada65e0942c57cfaae530a099d183ade03377955766c21707192cf534c57a7.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ff779d8-2902-5dbe-9b08-74d03fa4ab9b" :answer/id #uuid "7ff779d8-2902-5dbe-9b08-74d03fa4ab9b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/dc/dc641778741dd5fbe43c8edad8a34f033e898eb279a7fa680b44f75aaebaabda.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28df0d1a-86ac-5691-b099-21f856eccfac" :answer/id #uuid "28df0d1a-86ac-5691-b099-21f856eccfac" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/f2/f22991a62043a8fbd1e27ea16a103a830fa5e3874517e9c84d6b11f9dac0702e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198577 :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f899ac64f4d16cc14255603642e9038ec0b76f6b3ec27da53d3692b25431444.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186278345 :question/problem "![](images/c8/c86259b973101d17d732f4ac8c29a1b61de9fadfc67424a2ab6d28a2ceaa9a01.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b54a0f96-7cef-5900-9667-b793d1ee0611" :question/id #uuid "b54a0f96-7cef-5900-9667-b793d1ee0611" :question/math-academy-id "q-160947" :question/problem "![](images/16/1686ee5f02ab2215b700216f8dd0844d4763d0f3bde9837cb7913d2790a9c310.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=1$ and $u=\\frac{3}{2},$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b8/b82293b9940cff262ec5601f8652df8aa739fb7e3d1cf66a323cff4d1c46ff0f.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9bc5b6cd-4460-56f9-ace4-a472d1cd6181"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9bc5b6cd-4460-56f9-ace4-a472d1cd6181" :answer-field/id #uuid "9bc5b6cd-4460-56f9-ace4-a472d1cd6181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ded9cc47-a1a2-5ddf-be65-d06a1abaac16" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0e9607ce-f279-5606-a106-4f06825e56cf" "answer-2cfa80f7-dab2-569b-88e4-c82c59bd950f" "answer-06d4b6b0-a358-5d2b-800f-323c9e6650be" "answer-ded9cc47-a1a2-5ddf-be65-d06a1abaac16" "answer-a595837c-4b77-52a2-910f-992287910304"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e9607ce-f279-5606-a106-4f06825e56cf" :answer/id #uuid "0e9607ce-f279-5606-a106-4f06825e56cf" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/29/29f3ad97013b5bb588110ac919f3900801495b7f6a38ec99cae83388cc5e26d1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2cfa80f7-dab2-569b-88e4-c82c59bd950f" :answer/id #uuid "2cfa80f7-dab2-569b-88e4-c82c59bd950f" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/61/610bad214eeb3c16902775557b3f6badae765235fd6a8ec041f94974b114cf6c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06d4b6b0-a358-5d2b-800f-323c9e6650be" :answer/id #uuid "06d4b6b0-a358-5d2b-800f-323c9e6650be" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b4/b49ec400c11b5a840ef6616dea072b7dd983c75ffbaf7071b0abc6e946d437e0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ded9cc47-a1a2-5ddf-be65-d06a1abaac16" :answer/id #uuid "ded9cc47-a1a2-5ddf-be65-d06a1abaac16" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b8/b82293b9940cff262ec5601f8652df8aa739fb7e3d1cf66a323cff4d1c46ff0f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a595837c-4b77-52a2-910f-992287910304" :answer/id #uuid "a595837c-4b77-52a2-910f-992287910304" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/ea/eaae0415448f800d52eeb1fc13a927b41e9e9d180d1006ce4012d5d72a5864b0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186278387 :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=0$ and $u=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/83/832658a3d3a6add5f39be27844d2df16a07576ac8578b03b69ebac784b5b4c61.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198578 :question/problem "![](images/41/413ff5566eedb8e99c8fb0f3d29b8effd0e146ee4367db93264a5392edd818c1.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=1$ and $u=2,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b2/b221864b2795f2b158e8c4d1f88da23437f8d043da6c31e0b5c8392ebc1118bd.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186194099 :answer-field/correct 17592186194103 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186389146 :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=1$ and $u=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f9/f97f1344d6c3a0fd5cfbe06057193e4fe46f9d368cf32cc174db52ba94243744.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-9b6d5f3b-6c7c-587f-b086-0bc40be20551" :question/id #uuid "9b6d5f3b-6c7c-587f-b086-0bc40be20551" :question/math-academy-id "q-161159" :question/problem "![](images/38/383b3750be20f665159215eea6b0134c0da930f574e7ef12ff7127b78ac22acb.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $u=3,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{8},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/5e/5eb48411cf0c9efd97bc99a4c5f6edd064664c98eeef87ceda6d9909a5056eab.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-75f4502e-563a-563a-b13c-0f58a709c7ce"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-75f4502e-563a-563a-b13c-0f58a709c7ce" :answer-field/id #uuid "75f4502e-563a-563a-b13c-0f58a709c7ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-49e58033-a329-548c-abff-9edc218c7598" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ab6bd7f5-9b66-5353-af53-c30d3946e682" "answer-bb2c862b-d1d4-5b56-a1cd-8af893b2b99a" "answer-49e58033-a329-548c-abff-9edc218c7598" "answer-f5301a08-27d2-5bf8-a354-e253c4c2c7e6" "answer-b2e92430-5470-58ee-8c3d-b7ebea8e1e5e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab6bd7f5-9b66-5353-af53-c30d3946e682" :answer/id #uuid "ab6bd7f5-9b66-5353-af53-c30d3946e682" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/44/441279c1475c240da098572074b3a2f49e151e5260543770095898a543556c6c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb2c862b-d1d4-5b56-a1cd-8af893b2b99a" :answer/id #uuid "bb2c862b-d1d4-5b56-a1cd-8af893b2b99a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/5c/5ce8905501312d2cf64d2acf0e605e2e66d7fe6d0d28aa29335ab893521062b5.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49e58033-a329-548c-abff-9edc218c7598" :answer/id #uuid "49e58033-a329-548c-abff-9edc218c7598" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/5e/5eb48411cf0c9efd97bc99a4c5f6edd064664c98eeef87ceda6d9909a5056eab.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5301a08-27d2-5bf8-a354-e253c4c2c7e6" :answer/id #uuid "f5301a08-27d2-5bf8-a354-e253c4c2c7e6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/98/98eb493dc4a73d5fab02d2494285a337623f9502322cf9fe2690f52110d05eea.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2e92430-5470-58ee-8c3d-b7ebea8e1e5e" :answer/id #uuid "b2e92430-5470-58ee-8c3d-b7ebea8e1e5e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/fe/fe2e21582c6a283d82b37f45a19dfba6334b0d4664c39353fdff8dbcdcaddef6.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199463 :tutorial/content "Consider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5} \\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nA sketch of $\\Delta$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\n     \n\nTo find the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ onto the $xy$-plane, we need to transform each side (bottom, top, left, and right) of $\\Delta$ separately: \n\n![](images/06/062a3d1f13e8566770118650c92231ebe17f5eba2b4ae8d8b41722e33a72baff.png)\n\nIt can be shown that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$ So, let's proceed to find the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by mapping these four sides." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199475 :tutorial/content "![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\n  \n\nOur goal is to find the image of the region $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nLet's first find the images of the horizontal sides:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$ Substituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a line segment that lies on a line passing through the origin. The slope of this line must be\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{\\sin \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)}{\\cos \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)} = \\tan\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg),\n$$\n\nmeaning that the line makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. \nThe endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca644fa90870697e9cea1740c6432d43f682c650521217f19ea6f87c09cba0fd.png)\n\n- Similarly, we can find the image of the top side, where $v=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$\n\n![](images/11/118c9d18e4d8158014643771c9fa018b1b15da38184f693b89d07f9fadd3c748.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199476 :tutorial/content "![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\n  \n\nOur transformation $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the images of the vertical lines:\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2e0e5a723b7a9fdc860bcf3f619b6db4bdbec24c06bc22f3121a620080969b6c.png)\n\n- Similarly, we can find the image of the right side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1b9f163724e97d662192d5d274768b3e501843a2a2267adc5a3e1f5c6c9d230.png)\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=2,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/04/048606038e26fe79430899c284beff24921397879bb0ce5d46ab1f3653cab47e.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198481 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
